@anon.d1ff9934ece6813bf2a30f9ce237ed68.2560 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [2538 x i8] }> <{ [2538 x i8] c"# `ffi_const`\0A\0AThe tracking issue for this feature is: [#58328]\0A\0A------\0A\0AThe `#[ffi_const]` attribute applies clang's `const` attribute to foreign\0Afunctions declarations.\0A\0AThat is, `#[ffi_const]` functions shall have no effects except for its return\0Avalue, which can only depend on the values of the function parameters, and is\0Anot affected by changes to the observable state of the program.\0A\0AApplying the `#[ffi_const]` attribute to a function that violates these\0Arequirements is undefined behaviour.\0A\0AThis attribute enables Rust to perform common optimizations, like sub-expression\0Aelimination, and it can avoid emitting some calls in repeated invocations of the\0Afunction with the same argument values regardless of other operations being\0Aperformed in between these functions calls (as opposed to `#[ffi_pure]`\0Afunctions).\0A\0A## Pitfalls\0A\0AA `#[ffi_const]` function can only read global memory that would not affect\0Aits return value for the whole execution of the program (e.g. immutable global\0Amemory). `#[ffi_const]` functions are referentially-transparent and therefore\0Amore strict than `#[ffi_pure]` functions.\0A\0AA common pitfall involves applying the `#[ffi_const]` attribute to a\0Afunction that reads memory through pointer arguments which do not necessarily\0Apoint to immutable global memory.\0A\0AA `#[ffi_const]` function that returns unit has no effect on the abstract\0Amachine's state, and a `#[ffi_const]` function cannot be `#[ffi_pure]`.\0A\0AA `#[ffi_const]` function must not diverge, neither via a side effect (e.g. a\0Acall to `abort`) nor by infinite loops.\0A\0AWhen translating C headers to Rust FFI, it is worth verifying for which targets\0Athe `const` attribute is enabled in those headers, and using the appropriate\0A`cfg` macros in the Rust side to match those definitions. While the semantics of\0A`const` are implemented identically by many C and C++ compilers, e.g., clang,\0A[GCC], [ARM C/C++ compiler], [IBM ILE C/C++], etc. they are not necessarily\0Aimplemented in this way on all of them. It is therefore also worth verifying\0Athat the semantics of the C toolchain used to compile the binary being linked\0Aagainst are compatible with those of the `#[ffi_const]`.\0A\0A[#58328]: https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/58328\0A[ARM C/C++ compiler]: http://infocenter.arm.com/help/index.jsp?topic=/com.arm.doc.dui0491c/Cacgigch.html\0A[GCC]: https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/Common-Function-Attributes.html#index-const-function-attribute\0A[IBM ILE C/C++]: https://www.ibm.com/support/knowledgecenter/fr/ssw_ibm_i_71/rzarg/fn_attrib_const.htm\0A" }>, align 1
@anon.d1ff9934ece6813bf2a30f9ce237ed68.2561 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [8 x i8] }> <{ [8 x i8] c"ffi_pure" }>, align 1
@anon.d1ff9934ece6813bf2a30f9ce237ed68.2562 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [2569 x i8] }> <{ [2569 x i8] c"# `ffi_pure`\0A\0AThe tracking issue for this feature is: [#58329]\0A\0A------\0A\0AThe `#[ffi_pure]` attribute applies clang's `pure` attribute to foreign\0Afunctions declarations.\0A\0AThat is, `#[ffi_pure]` functions shall have no effects except for its return\0Avalue, which shall not change across two consecutive function calls with\0Athe same parameters.\0A\0AApplying the `#[ffi_pure]` attribute to a function that violates these\0Arequirements is undefined behavior.\0A\0AThis attribute enables Rust to perform common optimizations, like sub-expression\0Aelimination and loop optimizations. Some common examples of pure functions are\0A`strlen` or `memcmp`.\0A\0AThese optimizations are only applicable when the compiler can prove that no\0Aprogram state observable by the `#[ffi_pure]` function has changed between calls\0Aof the function, which could alter the result. See also the `#[ffi_const]`\0Aattribute, which provides stronger guarantees regarding the allowable behavior\0Aof a function, enabling further optimization.\0A\0A## Pitfalls\0A\0AA `#[ffi_pure]` function can read global memory through the function\0Aparameters (e.g. pointers), globals, etc. `#[ffi_pure]` functions are not\0Areferentially-transparent, and are therefore more relaxed than `#[ffi_const]`\0Afunctions.\0A\0AHowever, accessing global memory through volatile or atomic reads can violate the\0Arequirement that two consecutive function calls shall return the same value.\0A\0AA `pure` function that returns unit has no effect on the abstract machine's\0Astate.\0A\0AA `#[ffi_pure]` function must not diverge, neither via a side effect (e.g. a\0Acall to `abort`) nor by infinite loops.\0A\0AWhen translating C headers to Rust FFI, it is worth verifying for which targets\0Athe `pure` attribute is enabled in those headers, and using the appropriate\0A`cfg` macros in the Rust side to match those definitions. While the semantics of\0A`pure` are implemented identically by many C and C++ compilers, e.g., clang,\0A[GCC], [ARM C/C++ compiler], [IBM ILE C/C++], etc. they are not necessarily\0Aimplemented in this way on all of them. It is therefore also worth verifying\0Athat the semantics of the C toolchain used to compile the binary being linked\0Aagainst are compatible with those of the `#[ffi_pure]`.\0A\0A\0A[#58329]: https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/58329\0A[ARM C/C++ compiler]: http://infocenter.arm.com/help/index.jsp?topic=/com.arm.doc.dui0491c/Cacigdac.html\0A[GCC]: https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/Common-Function-Attributes.html#index-pure-function-attribute\0A[IBM ILE C/C++]: https://www.ibm.com/support/knowledgecenter/fr/ssw_ibm_i_71/rzarg/fn_attrib_pure.htm\0A" }>, align 1
